          "targetDoi": "10.1016/j.jenvrad.2017.04.011",
          "snippet": "The relationship between cognitive performance and extreme environmental conditions has been described before [27,28]. Particularly, evaluating time estimation during an overwintering in Antarctica involves the interaction of a wide range of variables that can shape this phenomenon, such as extreme weather [10], isolated and confined conditions [34], exposure to radiation [57], and mental health [35]. We assume that subjective time perception was not a result of multiple repetitions of the task during the year, since other studies have evidenced that time estimation, assayed through a 30s-production task in a non-polar region, showed virtually no changes in control subjects along a one-year period [53].",
          "section": "discussion",
